00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:40.730
Built-in libraries like this
are declared in app.yaml.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.740
Here, create a new
library section

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:45.140
with grpcio and setuptools.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:48.600
If your app's app.yaml
has a library section,

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.750
and these helper libraries,
if they're not already there.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.660
The good news is that
third party packages

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.670
are way easier in
the next gen, where

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.220
it's just requirements.txt.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.320
In the module 1
video, we explained

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.090
you need to tell
App Engine where

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.600
to look for your self bundle or
copied third party libraries.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.270
That's done in
appengine_config.py.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.940
It's also true of built-in
third party libraries,

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:14.330
like grbcio and setuptools,
but requires a different code

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.180
snippet in App Engine config,
where you reference them

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:20.360
to lib in the
setuptools.packageresources

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.970
working set, just
like you see here.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.130
This App Engine
config can now handle

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:28.260
both built-in and copied
third party libraries.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.380
So this is probably
pretty good boilerplate

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.170
to have for all of your
Python 2 App Engine apps.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.960
We mentioned earlier how
third party package management

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:39.830
is way easier in Python 3, and
not even having an App Engine

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:42.250
config file is part of that.

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:43.930
Next are the third
party packages

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.970
we do need to copy or bundle.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:49.330
In module 1, we created
requirements.txt

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:51.310
with a single
reference to Flask.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:55.900
Now add the Cloud NDB library
spelled google-cloud-ndb, all

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.250
lowercase with
hyphens in between.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:02.140
When making this video,
Flask was on version 1.1.2

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.810
and Cloud NDB was on 1.8.0.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.210
Since the repo is
actively maintained,

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:09.580
you may see newer
versions there.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.530
Now delete any
old lib folder you

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.720
may have and run the
pip install command

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:17.560
to get those libraries in there
so we can upload to App Engine.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:18.880
That's it for config.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.029
Let's jump into main.py,
starting with the imports.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.700
It's a one-for-one swap
to switch from App Engine

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.710
NDB to Cloud NDB.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:29.170
As with most Google
Cloud client libraries,

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:32.440
you'd need to create a
client to access the API.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.510
So that's the new line you see.

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:38.560
The best news is that most
of your NDB code stays as is.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.680
The only difference is that
it's more idiomatic Python,

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.830
requiring use of the
client's context manager.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.300
If you're new to
those, it's just

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.060
saying that you need to acquire
those resources before you

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:50.110
can use them.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:52.960
You'll typically see with
statements with files,

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:54.850
because you've got to
be able to open a file

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.910
before you can read from
or write to it, right?

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:01.660
So add a new with block and
put all of your Datastore calls

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.950
inside, like what you see here.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:05.830
Everything else is
the same, making

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:08.410
this migration fairly painless.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:11.410
One more tip is that
transactional tasks isn't

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.900
a feature in Cloud
NDB yet, meaning you

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:17.800
can't create tasks only if
the transaction succeeds.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:20.080
In other words, task
queue calls won't

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.630
get rolled back if
the transaction fails,

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:25.150
only Datastore operations.

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:28.960
Ignoring the readme, all other
source files stay unchanged.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.190
Index.html template file,
gcloudignore, and really,

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:33.940
that's it.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.180
So that's the entire
migration to Cloud NDB.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.040
Before we go back
to the presentation,

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:42.370
let's talk briefly
about a Python 3 port.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.460
The language updates you have
to make may be considerable.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.680
If there's any good news
on the App Engine side,

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.780
it mostly involves
deleting stuff,

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.360
like deleting a bunch
of lines from app.yaml,

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:56.230
deleting the lib folder and
appengine_config.py file.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:06:00.280
Our super simple app is
already Python 2/3 compatible.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:03.670
So the only file that
needs updating is app.yaml.

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.170
Change the language version.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:06.550
Now, that's obvious.

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:08.710
Next gen App Engine
requires a web framework

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:10.280
that does its own routing.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.160
So you have three options here.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:15.040
Set all script handlers to
auto, because the framework

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:16.840
does the routing now.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:20.110
Two, delete all script handlers,
because if everything is auto,

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:21.880
they're all meaningless, right?

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:25.210
Or three, replace handlers
with an entry point section

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.850
if you have specific
startup requirements.

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:30.340
Read more about these
options in the docs.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:32.890
Note, I only said
script handlers.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:36.190
Static file handling
stays as is.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.980
Lastly, I've already said
third party package management

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.070
in the next gen is way
simpler, allowing you to delete

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:43.930
the library section entirely.

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:47.080
Again, the real work for your
app is the language update.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.100
OK, back to the show.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:50.730
MARTIN: Wow.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.380
That was a pretty
