00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:53.920
And while you're
looking that up, why

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.578
is it so important to
migrate to Cloud NDB anyway?

00:07:57.578 --> 00:07:58.870
WESLEY CHUN: Of course, Martin.

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:01.357
Here are its library
and its docs links.

00:08:01.357 --> 00:08:02.732
As far as why it's
important, you

00:08:02.732 --> 00:08:05.560
know that modernizing your app
and making them more portable

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:08.260
is one goal of the latest
App Engine platform.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:10.270
For Python developers,
the key reason

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:14.470
is that Cloud NDB works in
both Python 2 and 3, which

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:16.210
means that once
you're on Cloud NDB,

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:19.570
you can finally upgrade
your app to Python 3.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.570
And with that, you've crossed
the datastore finish line

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:23.650
literally.

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:25.810
This is the last of the
required migrations,

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:27.580
as far as the Datastore goes.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:29.650
It's not necessary
to migrate further

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:32.620
to Cloud Datastore's native
client library nor Cloud

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:36.130
Firestore unless you've got
specific reasons to do so.

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:36.730
MARTIN: OK.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:38.140
What would be some
of the reasons

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:41.140
I'd consider those optional
Datastore migrations?

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:43.780
WESLEY CHUN: Well, using the
official Cloud Datastore client

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:47.230
library means moving away from
everything you know about NDB

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:48.520
and how it works.

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:50.740
The primary reason to
do this is if you have

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:52.420
other apps that already use it.

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:57.220
For example, Python 3 App Engine
apps or non App Engine apps.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:59.230
And you want to consolidate
so that you're not

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.150
using two different
client libraries

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:03.220
to talk to the same Datastore.

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:06.700
It simplifies and reduces
your maintenance costs too.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:08.260
Anyway, if you want
to look into it,

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:12.130
see the module 3
code lab and video.

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:15.610
The next generation of Cloud
Datastore is Cloud Firestore.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:17.800
Based on the name
change, you can probably

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:20.380
guess that it now has some
features from the Firebase

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:21.430
Realtime Database.

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:22.960
And you'd be right.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:24.970
This migration is
for those who must

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:27.760
have some of those Firebase
features for their apps.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.830
Check out module
6, if interested.

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:30.490
MARTIN: OK.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:33.760
So if those other Datastore
migrations are optional,

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.160
where can I go now?

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.910
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah,
great question, Martin.

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:39.730
Well, users should continue
to migrate from App Engine's

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:42.970
bundled services to standalone
services outside of App Engine,

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:44.440
like Cloud NDB.

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.680
What other App Engine services
and APIs are you using?

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:49.960
If you use push task
queues, consider

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:53.830
migrating to Cloud Tasks, and
see module 7 to get started.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:56.230
If your organization
is consolidating apps

00:09:56.230 --> 00:10:00.640
into containers for flexibility,
CI/CD, or consistency,

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:02.830
consider migrating to Cloud Run.

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:05.560
If so see, modules 4 and 5.

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:08.320
Finally, it goes without
saying that porting to Python 3

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:10.510
should always be a
consideration, since Python

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:12.280
2 has been sunset.

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:14.500
We add new migration
modules periodically,

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:16.060
and most will have videos too.

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:18.540
So keep checking back.

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:19.710
MARTIN: Cool.

